.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_destroy \- destroy a ccol_channel
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel_destroy(ch);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro frees all the resources of the ccol_channel. This includes both
of the queues inside it. The macro then sets the ccol_channel pointer to
NULL.

ccol_event_loop_remove() returns without a wait for a callback that it
already gave to a reactor thread. Such a callback can therefore still be
inside one of the queues inside the channel when the removal returns. This
call blocks until every one of those callbacks finishes with that queue.
Then it frees the queue. This is what makes the documented teardown
sequence safe as written. That sequence is: drain, remove, destroy.

A callback that the loop runs for a different registration can run this
whole sequence for this channel, with any num_reactor_threads. A dispatch
that the loop collected for it and did not start yet does not hold the
destroy up, and it never runs its callback after the removal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this with a NULL pointer.

The macro evaluates ch exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Messages that stay in one of the directions cause an assertion failure.
Drain both queues before you destroy the channel.
A watcher on one of the directions also causes an assertion failure. A
watcher is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an
ccol_event_loop registration (ccol_selectable_from_chan). Without that
assertion, the waiter is still linked and points to the mutex of the queue
below the channel, which the destroy is about to free.
Call ccol_event_loop_remove() before you destroy the channel. You can also
let every ccol_select() call and every ccol_select_timed() call that
watches the channel return first.
Do not call this from inside an ccol_event_loop callback that dispatches
one of the directions of this ccol_channel. This is a caller error, and
the program stops through ccol_fatal_err(). The destroy would wait for
that same callback.
Do not hold an application lock across this call if one of these callbacks
takes that lock. The destroy waits for the callback, and the callback
would wait for the lock.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_channel_create (3)
